{
	title: 'Tab commands run on the host; tab keys match in both clients',
	spec: `Tabs are host state, so typed /new, /close and /resume are host commands (src/host/commands/new.ts, close.ts, resume.ts), not clientOnly in the shared command list. They act on the session that ran them, so they work typed in the terminal or the web app and sent from another session. /new opens a tab right after that session, in its cwd, and shows it where that session shows (the go event). /close [tab number|session id] closes that session or the one named; /resume [session id] reopens the last closed tab or the one named, and shows it. Errors name the problem (not a tab, no session x).

A key press is not something the user wrote, so it adds nothing to history: Ctrl-T, Ctrl-W and Shift-Ctrl-T send tab-new, tab-close and tab-resume directly, as the tab bar does (terminal: src/client/commands/; web: src/web/tabs.ts). Only /quit, /suspend and /redraw stay clientOnly.

Both clients take their keys from one specification: command keys from the shared command list (common/commands/list.ts) and tab switching (Ctrl-N, Ctrl-P, Alt-1..0) from src/common/tab-keys.ts. The web has no key table of its own; it leaves only the keys the browser keeps (commandList.onWeb: Ctrl-T, Shift-Ctrl-T, Ctrl-W, Ctrl-N, Ctrl-P outside macOS) to the browser.`,
